  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/a/a2/X-Men_Vol_1_5.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "The X-Men arrive back home with heavy hearts, seeing as how their leader, Professor X, has lost his mind power (at the conclusion of the last issue).&nbsp; But they don't have much time to think about it, especially with the sudden arrival of Jean's parents, who've come to see the Professor and get a tour of the school (while on their way to the World's Fair), forcing the teenagers to quickly shed their X-Men uniforms.\n\nWhile touring the school, Scott is accidentally closed in the Danger Room, which immediately begins to attack him, apparently set for the Beast.&nbsp; But Scott is resourceful enough to survive until the end of the timed training period.&nbsp; He exits in time to participate in the tour the students give to Mr. and Mrs. Grey before they finally drive away.\n\nBut as their car rides off, we see Mastermind out, trying to find the X-Men, to no avail -- they seem to be too well hidden.&nbsp; His leader, Magneto, summons him back to \"Asteroid M\" -- a \"jagged asteroid, circling the Earth\" -- but it has a secret base on it where Magneto has his headquarters.\n\nAfter \"weeks of searching,\" they've been unable to find the X-Men, and since that teams is \"sworn to protect mankind,\" Magneto believes they must be taken out of the picture before the Evil Mutants can properly conquer the human race.&nbsp; He reminds all of them (particularly Quicksilver and the Scarlet Witch) that the humans \"would <i>destroy</i> you if they could!\"&nbsp; He has a plan, which requires the Toad's involvement.\n\nSoon, the scene shifts back to the X-Men, who are called in by Bobby to watch a track meet on TV.&nbsp; They note how one of the participants is effortlessly leaping over all the other contestants and winning virtually every event.&nbsp; They conclude that he <i>must</i> be a mutant!&nbsp; Even the crowd watching the event can tell something fishy is going on and call him a \"fake!\"&nbsp; The X-Men realize that he'll be mobbed by the crowd if they don't do something to intervene!&nbsp; As they leave, the Professor reminds them that he won't be able to intervene without his powers -- they'll be entirely on their own.\n\nThe X-Men arrive and hold off the mob long enough to escape into a subway where Hank finally has a chance to get a good look at the athlete and concludes that he <i>has</i> to be the Toad, who then reveals himself.&nbsp; They realize that they almost took him back to their secret headquarters, which would have revealed their location to Magneto!&nbsp; The Master of Magnetism then arrives on the scene at that moment, alongside the rest of the Brotherhood of Evil Mutants, and the two teams begin battling.&nbsp; Finally, Magneto snags the Angel by wrapping him in iron and orders his forces to withdraw.\n\n\n\nBack on the orbiting asteroid, Magneto tries to get Angel to reveal the location of the X-Men's headquarters, even shocking the Scarlet Witch by suggesting that \"only death will free you from me!\"&nbsp; She can't believe he would actually <i>murder</i> Angel.&nbsp; Back on Earth, the Toad seems like he's in a trance, like he can't believe Magneto would desert him, and he summons one of Magneto's \"drone pick-up capsules\" to take him back up to the asteroid, which all the other X-Men board as well.\n\nThe Brotherhood seem surprised when he returns, and the X-Men know that it's thus not a trap.&nbsp; But Magneto is able to control all the metal on the ship and prepares to eject them into space, only to see his control board short-circuited by the Scarlet Witch, who won't stand idly by!&nbsp; But even as Magneto turns to deal with the twins, the delay has allowed the X-Men to arrive.&nbsp; Cyclops detects the indecision by the pair and tries to reach out to them, but the hot-headed Quicksilver has no more love for the X-Men than he does Magneto.&nbsp; \"We can't trust <i>anyone</i>!\"\n\nBut at that moment, Magneto senses that the asteroid has gone off course and is starting to break up, and the last couple of pages are devoted to them all getting off of it without being destroyed.&nbsp; The X-Men manage to return to Earth in one of the drones but discover when they arrive that the drone is returning <i>somewhere</i> - Magneto must still be up there controlling it.&nbsp; \"Magneto can control its course <i>mentally</i>!&nbsp; There's no telling <i>where</i> he'll land!\"&nbsp; They realize they'll eventually be up against the evil mutants again someday.&nbsp; But for today, they've proved their mettle.\n\nWhen they return to the school, they find the Professor has been monitoring them mentally the whole time.&nbsp; It turns out that he never actually <i>lost</i> his powers.&nbsp; He reminds them that \"this is a <i>school</i>!&nbsp; And you can't graduate from any school without passing your final exam!\"&nbsp; They've just <i>taken</i> that exam - just as he planned it, and they've all passed.&nbsp; \"You've proven you can think and act for yourselves!&nbsp; Your training period is over!&nbsp; <i>Congratulations, my X-Men</i>!!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "The opening pages featuring the X-Men returning to their school with the injured Professor, their subsequent visit from Jean's parents, and a seemingly-meaningless distraction of Cyclops being trapped inside a Danger Room set to test the Beast -- all of is allows Jack Kirby to consume <b>six entire pages</b> before the narrative <i>finally</i> turns to Magneto's asteroid headquarters up in space -- what he refers to as \"Asteroid M.\"\n[ASSET: center | AsteroidM | width=large]\n\nIt's an <i>awfully</i> fancy hideout for an evil bad guy, but Magneto might actually be the only one powerful enough to have legitimately pulled this off, so I didn't find particularly outrageous, and as such, won't take any points off for the James Bond-like villain headquarters.&nbsp; (And for once, it's at least not another castle, like the one we've seen Dr. Doom hanging out in, not to mention several other, lower-tiered villains!)\n\nAt this early stage in Marvel's development, can you identify any other baddies out there that even <i>approach</i> the threat level of Magneto or Dr. Doom?&nbsp; (And don't count the Sub-Mariner, because I don't view him as being truly bad, so he's <i>disqualified</i>!)&nbsp; No, these two are <i>clearly</i> the cream of the crop when it comes to villains.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(This is even alluded to in the [LINK:1964:FF_27|FF #27|2027-07-04] letters/announcements page.)]\n\n[ASSET: left | WeeksOfSearching | width=14%] After the normal, space-wasting page of infighting between the members of the Brotherhood of Evil Mutants, Magneto finally gathers everyone together on page 8 to inform them of their new plan, one apparently made necessary because \"after weeks of searching,\" they've been unable to locate the X-Men.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=error; text=Once again, Stan and Jack demonstrate their inattention to detail when it comes to timing.]\n\nOn the splash page, it's made clear that the X-Men are just arriving home after their last adventure, with the now-powerless Professor X, yet by page 8, we're being told that Magneto and his evil cohorts haven't found any traces of the X-Men even \"weeks of searching.\"\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=What?]\n\nSo the story is telling us that it took \"weeks\" for the X-Men to return home?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; text=This is obviously a blunder], and the second one of this same type in a mere 5 issues (see the timeline problem I pointed out in my [LINK:1963:XMEN_2|XMEN #2|2027-01-17] review as well).\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | TrackStar | width=19%] [ASSET: right | HasToBeAHoax | width=19%] Magneto's plan to try and trick the X-Men into revealing the location of their secret headquarters (by using the Toad as bait, posing as a mutant track star) was sound enough.\n[BR]\n[BR] What is notable is that the angry mob never accuses the Toad of anything other than trying to pull off some sort of hoax (never once accusing him of being a mutant, even though the X-Men themselves seem to take it that way).\n\nOn the surface, having the X-Men show up to defend one who seems to have cheated in some manner [SPAN: severity=warning; text=is a problem.]&nbsp; But let's not forget that the newspaper Johnny Storm was reading at the beginning of last month's [LINK:1964:ST_120_torch1|ST #120torch|2027-06-08], which had a headline story about the team and even revealed that (quoting from its splash page) \"They say the X-Men are all <i>mutants!</i>  They were <i>born</i> with their power instead of getting it <i>accidentally</i> like <i>we</i> did!\"\n\n[HANG0] So the only conclusion we can draw is that\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=even as early as this issue, the public has already been informed that the X-Men are mutants.]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n\nI give credit to Stan and Jack for the pacing, once past the book's initial pages.&nbsp; From the time the Toad appeared on the scene in order to lure the X-Men out of hiding, to the time Magneto took off with the Angel, a surprising seven pages had elapsed, and I was actually surprised when I looked down at the page number and found that it said 16.&nbsp; I normally find fight scenes overly tedious, but in this case, Jack did an excellent job weaving in the various characters to maintain interest.\n\n\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\nThe Brotherhood of Evil Mutants is interesting enough, and the introduction seems to have gone over so well with the readers that Stan and Jack decided to bring them back in the very next issue (at least according to the splash page -- this is a bi-monthly book after all, so it's plausible).&nbsp; The band of characters Magneto has pulled together <i>clearly</i> has no love lost between them, and all simply serve Magneto out of fear (Toad), obligation (Quicksilver/Scarlet Witch) or convenience (Mastermind).\n\nWhat seems just as clear is that Magneto has <i>zero</i> regard for their lives, pointing out time and again that he's the strongest mutant -- thus they <i>better</i> obey!&nbsp; Page 15 even has him say, \"Forget the Toad!&nbsp; <i>Run!</i>  We have the <i>Angel</i>!&nbsp; I'll swap him for that high-jumping fool <i>any</i> day!\"\n\nI'm sure such sentiments must really <i>endear</i> him to his followers -- [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=NOT!]\n\nReaders are apparently expected to sympathize with the twins, and even Cyclops can sense how torn they are to be associated with Magneto.&nbsp; I award positive points for the characterization of the Evil Mutants, if not for the rationale of them staying together.\n\n\nThis story gets major points off for the seeming disregard that the Professor had for their lives in his \"final exam.\"&nbsp; It's a case of \"you either pass the exam or you <i>die</i>!\"&nbsp; This is a school, so one would assume that a momentary failure would be turned into a \"teaching moment,\" and not death.&nbsp; So I'm sorry, but that did <i>not</i> work for me at all!&nbsp; And lest someone think that perhaps he could have intervened to <i>prevent</i> a death, what on Earth could he have done from the ground while his X-Men were up in space on the asteroid?&nbsp; The whole thing comes across as very callous.&nbsp; The Professor is all smiles at the end of the issue, but boy, what were his X-Men <i>thinking</i> after that, after he gambled their lives for the sake of a test?\n\n\n[ASSET: left | CyclopsNoHandsPowerBeam | width=12%] On page 15, Cyclops is holding the Toad with both arms, yet is still able to shoot his eye beams at Quicksilver, who thinks \"I didn't realize he could use his power beam while I was holding his arms!\"&nbsp; Interesting, because I'm almost certain that at this early stage, Cyclops <i>did</i> have to manipulate his visor with a hand.&nbsp; I'll have to pay attention going forward...\n\nFinally, I note that once again, [SPAN: severity=error; text=the Scarlet Witch is anything but scarlet on the cover], but perhaps it's because both here and in issue #4, she's standing either behind or just beside Magneto, whose costume is [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=red].&nbsp; Perhaps the colorist thought that they needed to be different colors to avoid blending together, even though on the <i>inside</i>, her costume leans more toward pink than Magneto's solid red one.&nbsp; Still, it's visually jarring to see a \"Scarlet\" Witch dressed in green on both covers.&nbsp; (<i>Heh</i>, and it seems I'm not the <i>only</i> one to have noticed this, as evidenced by Rocky Midyett's letter in XMEN #7 where he says [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"By the way, if the Scarlet Witch is scarlet, how come she's always green on the cover?\"])&nbsp; As for Quicksilver, he's <i>also</i> discolored on the cover, being blue rather than green.\n\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\n<b>FIXME</b>",
  "side_note": [
    "[HANG0] Mr. and Mrs. Grey stop by the school on their way to the World's Fair, which <i>was</i> indeed held in New York City in 1964.&nbsp; This is not the first time the event has been mentioned (see [LINK:1963:TOS_45_iron0|TOS #45] as well).\n\n[HANG0] Hank's glove situation has not changed.&nbsp; He's sometimes drawn <i>with</i> them (e.g. the splash page), but most of the time <i>without</i>.&nbsp; Back and forth it goes throughout the issue...&nbsp; This might simply be the way it's going to be in all the X-Men issues -- I have no idea.&nbsp; It's just something to keep an eye on, but if it's going to happen this frequently, I may stop calling it out entirely."
  ],
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}